\DOC ASSUME_CALLEE_SAFETY_TAILED_TAC

\TYPE {ASSUME_CALLEE_SAFETY_TAILED_TAC : thm -> string -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Adds a labelled assumption derived from a supplied safety theorem,
instantiating its existential event-function witness to a freshly named
function and generalising its event trace over an extra trailing-events
variable, leaving the goal's conclusion unchanged.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument {safety_th : thm} is the callee safety theorem — an
existentially quantified {ensures} statement — whose body becomes the new
assumption; it may be given by any theorem-producing expression. The second
argument {name : string} is the label attached to the new assumption. The added
assumption {u} is the universally quantified body of the theorem argument, in
which the existential witness is replaced by a fresh concrete function constant
whose name carries an index, one extra universally quantified variable {z} for
a trailing event list is appended at the end of the bound-variable list
{x1 ... xn}, and the two occurrences of the initial event trace in the
precondition and postcondition are replaced by that trace appended onto {z}.
Where the theorem is a subroutine-style statement, the assumption's state
components may appear in an expanded or rewritten form relative to the goal's
conclusion.
{
               A ?- t
   ===============================  ASSUME_CALLEE_SAFETY_TAILED_TAC safety_th name
        A, name: u ?- t
}
\noindent Existing assumptions {A} keep their labels unchanged.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces the tactic leaves one goal and only ever adds an
assumption, never removing or altering others.

\ENDDOC
